    {
      "object_id": "mathia_interpretation_6caec6df798f6e632a2e692903780d718624106c6b5f83a558a8fb391806bd0f",
      "object_role": "interpretation",
      "proposed_content": "The ternary Goldbach result combines distinct kinds of rigor without the span specifying a clean complement-of-ranges mechanism. Helfgott's improved major- and minor-arc estimates yielded the unconditional weak Goldbach theorem, and his proof used Platt's interval-arithmetic verification of RH for a bounded collection and height range of Dirichlet L-functions as a finite analytic input. This shows that certified computation can enter an analytic proof as a rigorously delimited ingredient, but the exact interface between that ingredient and the circle-method estimates remains unstated here.",
