    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_b1cc7eb24fe067766d0e6d8051380fba2af020e4a6ed6155663c5378edcda229",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The source abstracts Riemann's method as a chain of representations: pass from erratic arithmetic data to summatory functions, encode coefficients by a Dirichlet series, invert with a Perron integral, and use analytic continuation when attempting to shift contours. Zeta-type functions extend this strategy to residue classes and number fields through compatible Dirichlet-series, Euler-product, continuation, and functional-equation views, while the Goldbach example shows that bounded L-function zero certification can enter a theorem as a delimited analytic input.",
