    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_e20fba91422e09ce128d8f57750be6fab59375bf1c7b25c99de414e56047afb8",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The pi-versus-Li and Mertens histories exhibit the same methodological failure of finite extrapolation: behavior can persist over enormous observed ranges while spectral oscillations eventually overturn the proposed global bound. Explicit zero expansions make the hidden oscillations actionable, and in the Mertens case simultaneous phase alignment plus LLL replaces infeasible domain scanning; the survey's iterated-log warning explains more generally why finite plots can conceal asymptotic change.",
